WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:09.740
And now we turn to the next topic, which will

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:16.740
reveal to us that some hidden messages are
more surprising than others. After we implemented

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:22.540
the Frequent Words Problem, let's apply it
to real genomes and start from the replication

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:28.439
origin in the bacterium Vibrio cholerae.
And when we run our algorithms, it turns out

00:00:28.439 --> 00:00:35.439
there are too many potential replication origins.
We actually find 4 frequent words.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:40.790
Not to mention that we do not know yet whether
it's even statistically surprising to find

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:46.069
a 9-mer in a string of length 500 that appears
3 or more times.

00:00:46.069 --> 00:00:51.850
So, which of these four [frequent words] represents
a real DnaA box, and which of them are just

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:53.410
statistical artifacts?

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:59.439
Well, in this case, it's actually possible
to figure out which of these 4 candidates

00:00:59.439 --> 00:01:04.459
represents a real DnaA box because two of
them are very special.

00:01:04.459 --> 00:01:11.459
They represent reverse complementary k-mers.
And here I showed that one of them actually

00:01:12.689 --> 00:01:17.850
is a perfect reverse complement to another.
You may think that I am cheating because if

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:24.850
you look at the red 9-mers shown in the box
and the red 9-mer shown in the bottom slide

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:34.790
of this slide, they are different.
But remember, DNA has a direction, and strands

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:39.040
run in opposite directions.
So when we look at the string starting from

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:44.730
TAC (red string) we should actually read it
in the opposite direction, and then everything

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:49.259
would make sense.
So, hidden message found!...

00:01:49.259 --> 00:01:56.259
And it is extremely surprising, almost unbelievable,
to find one word appearing six times, either

00:01:57.519 --> 00:02:01.649
as itself, or as a reverse complement in a
short string.

00:02:01.649 --> 00:02:06.310
So it reinforces our belief that we are onto
something.

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:11.550
And after we have done it, let's try something
else. Let's try to find hidden message in

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:18.550
Thermotoga petrophila, which is a bacteria
enjoying life in very hot environment, about

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:23.959
80 degrees Celsius.
Maybe we should simply look for these two

00:02:23.959 --> 00:02:29.999
k-mers that we found before, but unfortunately,
they are not even there.

00:02:29.999 --> 00:02:36.509
So, it looks like we need to run our Frequent
Words Problem again, and in this case we actually

00:02:36.509 --> 00:02:43.509
find 6 frequent words, which tells us that
probably different genomes have different

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:47.439
hidden messages.

00:02:47.439 --> 00:02:52.829
It complicates our life quite a lot because
for thousands of bacteria, we need to search

00:02:52.829 --> 00:02:57.319
for newer and newer hidden messages.
But with the Frequent Words Problem, we are

00:02:57.319 --> 00:03:02.290
at least armed with algorithms to do it for
any bacterium.

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:09.259
Now, let' consult with Ori-Finder to choose
which one of these six 9-mers represents the

00:03:09.259 --> 00:03:15.589
real DnaA box.
It tells us that it looks like the one shown

00:03:15.589 --> 00:03:22.589
on this slide.
And we learned therefore how to find frequent

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:30.540
words and hopefully DnaA boxes in any bacteria.
But wait a second, I told you how to find

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:39.490
DnaA boxes as frequent words if the origin
of replication is known.

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:45.999
But of course, the position of the origin
of replication, this 500-nucleotide region

00:03:45.999 --> 00:03:52.999
where we look for frequent words, is unknown.
How possibly can we find it?

00:03:53.139 --> 00:03:59.680
Remember, we have to find this 500-nucleotide
region in a genome that may be millions of

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:01.910
nucleotides long.

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:08.910
And, let's resort to the idea that I call
"clumps" or "clusters" of hidden message to

00:04:09.189 --> 00:04:12.689
solve this problem.
So, what we have been doing before?

00:04:12.689 --> 00:04:18.600
We assumed that the replication origin region
that may be 500 nucleotides long is known.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:24.689
And in this replication origin, we were finding
frequent words, for example, in this window

00:04:24.689 --> 00:04:31.689
we found a frequent word that appears 4 times,
so it gives us a hint that maybe it is a DnaA

00:04:34.149 --> 00:04:36.850
box.
But if we don't know the replication origin,

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:41.629
what should we do?
Maybe we should look at all possible windows

00:04:41.629 --> 00:04:48.629
in the genome, and if in a particular window,
we find some frequent words appearing many

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:54.839
times, it's a hint that maybe we found a replication
origin.

00:04:54.839 --> 00:05:01.300
Let's define "short" as something of length
L and many as "at least t times" and that's

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:08.100
defines the notion of an (L,t)-clump -- it's
a k-mer that appears in a window of length

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:14.040
L at least t times.
And now you have a new Rosalind challenge

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:18.499
which is the Clump Finding Problem.
You are a given a string called Genome, an

00:05:18.499 --> 00:05:25.499
integer k (the length of k-mer), an integer
L (window length), and t (number of patterns

00:05:25.610 --> 00:05:31.899
in a clump).
In Output, you have to find all (L,t)-clumps

00:05:31.899 --> 00:05:32.829
in the genome.

00:05:32.829 --> 00:05:38.589
And we hope that every (L,t)-clump will point
us to the location of replication origin.

00:05:38.589 --> 00:05:44.100
Let's assume that you solved it and let's
run it. Let's run it on E. coli genome, the

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:51.100
workhorse of bacterial genomics.
Here is the result: we find about two thousand

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:58.700
potential (500,3)-clumps, which means 500-length
windows where there are frequent words appearing

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:04.570
3 or more times.
It is just too many. It's absolutely unclear

00:06:04.570 --> 00:06:09.550
how to choose the real replication origin
among them.

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:16.550
What should we do? Should we give up? Let's
try to develop new ideas and see how we can

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:19.570
move further despite the fact that we seem
to be stuck.

